  "updated": "2026-08-19T09:51:54.446194+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..The combination of ample moisture moving into \nSoutheast Arizona from the Gulf of California and a more \nfavorable weather pattern aloft will result in an active \nMonsoon thunderstorm day across all of Southeast Arizona. \nExpect storms to initially develop over the Gila National Forest\nof New Mexico and the higher terrain of northern Graham and \nnorthern Greenlee counties around mid-day, then drive southwest \ntoward the valleys of Southeast Arizona through the afternoon, \nthen across central and western Pima county this evening. The \nprimary concern with storms today is that they will be capable \nof producing organized storm outflow winds to 45-55 mph, \npotentially producing localized dense blowing dust. A Blowing \nDust Advisory is in effect from 3 PM MST this afternoon through \n10 PM MST this evening for the western portions of Southeast \nArizona. Deep moisture moving into the region from the Gulf of \nCalifornia (gulf surge) will bring about a secondary concern of \nheavy rainfall capable of producing isolated flash flooding, \nespecially across central and eastern Pima county. \n\nThe upper pattern this morning is defined by a 500 mb high \ncenter over NW AZ, and a SW-NE tilted 500-300 mb ridge axis \nextending from the NRN Baja Peninsula northeast into NW AZ. \nThis ridge axis will move into NRN Arizona today and become \noriented along a W-E axis. This will result in a modest area of\ndeformation/lift aloft along a general axis extending from \nSafford to Sells. In addition, the latest GOES precipitable \nwater imagery indicated a gulf surge was making its way into SW \nAZ this morning, with PWAT values between 1.50 and 1.75 inches \nacross central and western Pima/WRN Pinal and Maricopa counties.\nSurface dewpoints have been on the increase west of Tucson, \nwith values ranging from the mid 60s to lower 70s. An even  \ndeeper area of moisture with PWATs values between 1.80 and 2.00\ninches was currently in the NRN gulf of California, and should \nadvect into the lower deserts through the day today. With the \nstrong heating the past several days along with this deep \nmoisture, MUCAPEs between 1500-2000 J/kg will be available \nacross the western half of SE AZ this afternoon. The drier sub- \ncloud layer SE and S of Tucson today will initially provide the\nopportunity for strong outflow winds of 45-55 mph. These \noutflows will generally kick to the west and northwest into the \nevening. Given the high CAPEs available, these boundaries will \ninitiate additional convection along them with new development \nre-inforcing the outflows. This is supported by the 19/00Z HREF \nwith 10-20% neighborhood probabilities of storms outflows 50+ \nkts across Santa Cruz/central and ERN Pima and SE Pinal counties\nbetween 3-10 PM MST. Given this, we thought it would be \nnecessary to hoist a Blowing Dust Advisory for dense blowing \ndust and the potential for a haboob formation this afternoon \nimpacting mainly Pima and SE Pinal counties. \n\nThe NWLY/WLY flow in the lower-mid levels will push this deeper\nmoisture into the mid-levels of storms today, increasing the \npotential for heavy rainfall across central and eastern Pima \ncounties (including the Tohono O'odham Nation and the Tucson \nMetro Area). Coordinated with the WPC this morning to elevate \nthese areas into their SLGT category on their Day 1 Excessive \nRainfall Outlook (ERO) for today.\n\nOtherwise, we will remain in an active weather pattern into the\nweekend as the upper high center moves toward the Four Corners \nThursday and Friday, then into the Rockies by Saturday afternoon.\nThe Monsoon season rolls on.",
